  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThe forecast continues to be very straight-forward through the \nnext week, and confidence is therefore high in the forecast \ndetails. Global model ensembles agree that an anomalously warm \nupper level ridge will slowly shift eastward across the lower \nMississippi Valley region as we finish up the week and go into \nnext week. This will lead to a continuation of dry weather with \nlarge diurnal temperature ranges approaching 40 deg F.\n\nOf greatest importance, the continued drying of vegetation \ncombined with exceptionally low daytime RH values will help to \nmaintain an elevated fire danger threat. We will continue to \nmessage this through the graphics and fire danger statement. Wind \nshould have less influence and be a smaller factor in determining \nthe fire danger threat levels.\n\nOtherwise, rainfall is not expected until the end of next week at\nthe earliest, when the ridge shows signs of breaking down, and \nwhen the subtropical stream influence increases over our region.\nEven then, global ensembles show the greater rainfall signal \ncentered more to our west, so confidence is not great for this dry\nspell to end anytime soon. /EC/",
